<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kirkwall</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kirkwall is the main town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, on the island of Mainland in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. It grew around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-magnus-cathedral">St Magnus Cathedral</a>, begun in 1137, and became the seat of the earls and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Orkney, whose palaces stand beside the cathedral. It was made a royal burgh in 1486 after the islands passed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> to Scotland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kirkwall.jpg" alt="Kirkwall" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stevekeiretsu · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kirkwall_Harbour.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kirkwall stands on the northern coast of Mainland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, with its harbours in the bay of Kirkwall to the north and Scapa Flow 2.3 kilometres to the south. It is 210 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a> and 850 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. Its parish, St Ola, forms the isthmus between Firth and Holm. On the western edge of the town lies the Grain Earth House, a prehistoric underground passage leading to a small pillared chamber. Such <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> earth houses, or souterrains, are characteristic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-isles">Northern Isles</a>, though their purpose remains unknown.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name comes from the Norse Kirkjuvágr, meaning church bay, the settlement having been established by the Norsemen in the 11th century. It is first mentioned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkneyinga-saga">Orkneyinga saga</a> in 1046, as the residence of Rögnvald Brusason, Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, who was killed by his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thorfinn-the-mighty">Thorfinn the Mighty</a>. The church of the name was not the cathedral, which was originally at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birsay">Birsay</a>, but an 11th-century church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-ii-of-norway">Saint Olaf</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-magnus-cathedral">St Magnus Cathedral</a> was founded in memory of Saint Magnus Erlendsson, Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> from 1108 to 1117, by Earl Rögnvald Kali, later a saint himself. Beside it stand the ruins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a>'s Palace and the Earl's Palace. In 1486 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-iii-of-scotland">James III of Scotland</a> made Kirkwall a royal burgh, and it later returned a commissioner to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-scotland">Parliament of Scotland</a>. After the Union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> it joined other towns to form the Northern Burghs, which sent one member to Parliament, and in 1918 the burgh merged into the constituency of Orkney and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shetland">Shetland</a>.</p><p>Steamer services to Kirkwall were run from 1836 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leith">Leith</a>, Clyde and Tay Shipping Company. Kirkwall Grammar School has existed since about 1200. The town has many houses and other buildings of the 17th and 18th centuries in the local style, and the town hall was completed in 1884. Tankerness House, one of the best-preserved 16th-century town houses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, holds the Orkney Museum.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kirkwall is the largest town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, with 9,293 inhabitants at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and the seat of Orkney Islands Council. It is technically a cathedral city. Ferries run from its harbour to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a>, Lerwick and the northern islands, and cruise ships call there. The St Magnus International Festival, founded with the help of the composer Peter Maxwell Davies, is held each midsummer, and the Ba Game is played between the Uppies and the Doonies on Christmas Day and New Year's Day.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kirkwall” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Earl Magnus, murdered around 1117, was venerated as a saint, and his nephew Rögnvald Kali Kolsson began St Magnus Cathedral in <b>Kirkwall</b> in 1137.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-orkney">Earldom of Orkney</a></li></ul></section>
